DO  - 10.1186/s12966-026-01958-1
UR  - https://doi.org/10.1186/s12966-026-01958-1
AN  - PMID:42968090
AB  - Background Inadequate physical activity (PA) elevates chronic disease risk in low-income urban populations. Neighborhood parks may offer venues for PA; but effects likely depend on park quality and use patterns. This study examined whether citywide park renovations were associated with changes in adult PA and sedentary behavior (SB) in low-income New York City neighborhoods and whether associations between park use and PA/SB changes differed for residents near renovated vs. control parks. Methods We conducted a quasi‑experimental prospective cohort study (2016–2022) in neighborhoods surrounding 31 renovated and 21 matched control parks. Participants included 344 adults living ≤ 0.3 miles of a study park (186 intervention; 158 control). Outcomes were accelerometry‑based PA/SB measures collected pre‑ and post‑renovation: vector magnitude per minute (VMPM) and minutes/day of sedentary time; light PA; and moderate‑to‑vigorous PA. Surveys assessed self‑reported typical frequency and duration of park visits. Linear mixed‑effects difference-in-differences models estimated associations between renovations and PA/SB changes; interaction models examined associations of park use with PA/SB changes in intervention vs. control groups. Results Changes in PA/SB were not significantly different between intervention and control groups. However; intervention participants who reported visiting their study park ≥ 30 min on a weekend day experienced a 140.0-unit/day greater increase in VMPM (95% CI 52.7;227.2) and a 61.1-minute/day larger decrease in sedentary time (95% CI -108.8;-13.3) compared to those with shorter visit durations. Furthermore; in the intervention group; each additional 60 min spent at a study park on weekdays was associated with 18.0 more minutes/day of light PA (95% CI 4.7;31.3); and each additional 60 min spent at the study park on weekend days corresponded to 19.0 more minutes/day of light PA (95% CI 8.1;29.9) and a 54.1-unit increase in VMPM/day (95% CI 7.5;100.7). No associations between park use duration and PA/SB changes were observed in the control group. Conclusions Park renovations alone were not associated with PA/SB changes; but longer duration of visits at renovated—but not control—parks were associated with increased total PA volume and light PA; and decreased sedentary time. High-quality park environments may be a necessary pre-condition to the benefits of park use on adult PA/SB.
